  "for": "Anyone in a relationship (and psych, neuro and premed students). The playful hook names a feeling everyone has had; the video shows the real brain and hormone chain behind snapping at someone you love, with one calm thing to do about it. They send it to their partner as a tease that also says 'this is us'.",
  "title": "This is why you hate your boyfriend: the brain and hormones behind snapping",
  "question": "Hungry, tired, or stressed: what makes you snap fastest?",
  "send": "Send this to your boyfriend 😤",
  "music": "ambient-3",
  "hashtags": [
   "#psychology",
   "#neuroscience",
   "#relationships",
   "#brain",
   "#premed"
  ],
  "credit": "3D model: Z-Anatomy / BodyParts3D (CC BY-SA 4.0), remeshed in Blender for Anatomy Ace",
  "why": "The amygdala flags a threat and signals the hypothalamus, which fires sympathetic nerves straight to the adrenal medulla: adrenaline and noradrenaline within seconds (heart rate and blood pressure up). The slower HPA axis follows: CRH from the hypothalamus, ACTH from the anterior pituitary, cortisol from the adrenal cortex, peaking about 20-40 minutes after a stressor starts (Dickerson & Kemeny 2004). The hippocampus stores memories with their context, and the amygdala strengthens emotional ones (McGaugh 2004). The prefrontal cortex regulates the amygdala (reappraisal studies, Buhle et al. 2014). After one night without sleep the amygdala responded about 60% more to negative images and was less coupled to the medial prefrontal cortex (Yoo et al. 2007). Everyday hunger goes with more anger and irritability (Swami et al. 2022), and putting a feeling into words lowers the amygdala's response (Lieberman et al. 2007).",
